                <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic border-l-4 border-[#38BDF8] pl-6 bg-gray-50 py-4 rounded-r-xl">
                    Interior doors shape the entire feel of a room, yet most homeowners spend far more time deliberating over paint swatches than they do choosing the right door style. A barn door and a French door solve fundamentally different problems, and picking the wrong one can waste thousands of dollars while leaving you with a room that never quite works.
                </p>

                <p>
                    The barn door trend exploded around 2015, and a decade later it remains one of the most polarizing design choices in residential interiors. Proponents love the rustic character, the space-saving slide mechanism, and the visual drama a large panel of reclaimed wood introduces to an otherwise plain hallway. Critics point to the obvious functional gaps: barn doors do not seal against the frame, they leave visible gaps along all four edges, and they provide almost no sound insulation. Meanwhile, French doors have been a staple of Western architecture since the seventeenth century, prized for their ability to flood adjacent rooms with natural light while maintaining a genuine physical and acoustic barrier.
                </p>

                <p>
