                </p>

                <h2 id="comparison" class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
                    How Barn Doors Work
                </h2>
                <p>
                    A barn door is a flat panel that hangs from an exposed overhead track and slides laterally to open and close. The door does not swing into or out of the room. Instead, it glides along the wall surface adjacent to the doorway. The hardware system consists of a steel rail mounted above the door opening, roller hangers bolted through the top edge of the door panel, and a floor guide at the bottom that prevents the door from swinging outward. Most residential barn door tracks are six to eight feet long, though custom tracks can span much wider openings.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Because the door panel slides along the exterior face of the wall rather than fitting into a recessed pocket, there is always a gap between the door and the wall surface. This gap typically measures between one-half inch and one full inch depending on the hardware system and the flatness of the wall. There are also visible gaps at the top and bottom of the door. The top gap exists because the rollers require clearance, and the bottom gap exists because barn doors hang suspended from the track rather than resting on the floor. These gaps are the root cause of virtually every functional complaint about barn doors: they allow light, sound, air, and odors to pass freely around the door perimeter.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Modern residential barn door panels come in a wide variety of materials. Solid wood planks in reclaimed pine, alder, or oak are the most traditional and visually striking option, typically weighing between 60 and 100 pounds for a standard 36-by-84-inch panel. Hollow-core panels with a decorative veneer are lighter and less expensive but feel cheap and provide essentially zero sound dampening. Medium-density fiberboard panels with a painted finish offer a middle ground between weight and cost. There are also glass-paneled barn doors, often with a steel or iron frame, that mimic the transparency of French doors while keeping the sliding mechanism.
                </p>

                <h2 id="how-french-doors-work" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
                    How French Doors Work
                </h2>
                <p>
                    French doors consist of two hinged panels that swing open from the center of the doorway, each panel typically featuring multiple glass panes divided by wooden or metal muntins. The doors mount on traditional butt hinges or concealed European-style hinges attached to the left and right jambs of a finished door frame. When closed, the two panels meet at a center point, usually with an astragal strip or T-molding that seals the gap between them. Weatherstripping along all four edges of each panel and a threshold at the bottom complete the seal against drafts, sound, and light.
                </p>

                <p>
                    Standard residential French doors measure 60 inches wide for a pair of 30-inch panels, or 72 inches wide for a pair of 36-inch panels. The glass panes can be true divided lights where each small pane is a separate piece of glass set in individual muntin bars, or simulated divided lights where a single large pane has a decorative grid applied to one or both surfaces. True divided lights are more expensive and heavier but offer a more authentic appearance and are easier to repair if a single pane breaks. Simulated divided lights are lighter, cheaper, and provide better insulation because the single large pane can be double-glazed.
                </p>

                <p>
                    The primary functional advantage of French doors over barn doors is the complete seal. When properly installed with quality weatherstripping and a threshold, French doors create a genuine barrier between two spaces. This barrier blocks sound transmission, prevents air exchange between heated and unheated rooms, stops light from bleeding through, and provides real privacy. French doors can also accept a standard latch or deadbolt lockset, giving them a level of security that barn doors simply cannot achieve with their exposed sliding hardware.
                </p>

