                <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
                <div class="mt-16">
                  <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
                    Common Mistakes When Choosing Between Barn and French Doors
                  </h2>

                  <p>
                    Both styles photograph beautifully and behave very differently once installed. The regrets almost always come from a practical constraint that was not measured beforehand.
                  </p>

                  <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
                    <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
                      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting the Barn Door Needs a Wall to Slide Onto</h3>
                      <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                        A sliding barn door needs clear wall space at least as wide as the door itself, with no light switches, outlets, artwork, or return walls in the way. This is the single most common discovery made after the hardware arrives. Measure the open position, not just the opening.
                      </p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
                      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing a Barn Door Where Privacy or Sound Matters</h3>
                      <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                        Barn doors hang in front of the opening rather than sealing into a jamb, leaving a gap around the perimeter. They pass sound and light freely, which rules them out for bedrooms, home offices on calls, and most bathrooms regardless of how good they look.
                      </p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
                      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the Swing Arc of French Doors</h3>
                      <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                        A pair of French doors needs the full depth of the door leaf clear on the swing side, and that arc quietly consumes floor space you may be counting on for furniture. In a tight room the doors end up permanently open against a wall, which defeats the purpose.
                      </p>
                    </div>
                    <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
                      <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Under-Specifying the Header for a Barn Door</h3>
                      <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                        Barn door track carries the entire weight of a solid slab on a handful of fixings, and drywall alone will not hold it. The track needs to land in solid blocking or a header spanning the studs. Skipping that step is how tracks sag and pull loose a year later.
                      </p>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                </div>

                <!-- Closing -->
                <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
                  <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
                  <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
                    The <strong class="text-white">barn door versus French door</strong> choice is really a question about the room. Barn doors save swing space and suit openings between living areas where sound and privacy do not matter. French doors seal properly, let light through in both directions, and look right on studies, dining rooms, and anywhere a real closure is needed.
                  </p>
                  <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
                    Measure before you decide: clear wall space for the slide, clear floor space for the swing, and solid backing for whichever hardware you choose. Both doors work well in the right opening and are a permanent irritation in the wrong one.
                  </p>
                </div>

